@packages/compiler/src/adapter.sigil import { SigilAgentAdapter }

expand SigilAgentAdapter {
  logic {
    AgentAdapter {
      Supply each adapter the target request, evaluation skill, output schema,
      workspace root, and requested capabilities for its evaluation run.

      Resolve exactly one registered adapter matching the effective profile's
      provider, implementation identifier, and exact implementation version. Reject
      zero or multiple matches before evaluation.

      Invoke the selected CLI with the restrictions supported and declared by that
      adapter. Consume structured observations when available, or wait for a terminal
      structured result when progress is unavailable.

      Stream-process provider output. Omit or truncate bulky command output without
      failing an otherwise valid evaluation, while preserving enough framing to
      locate and validate the required final result.

      A valid-result terminal transition is observable only after the provider
      process terminates and every designated output channel reaches end-of-stream.
      Parse the complete owned stream and require exactly one terminal candidate.

      Validate the complete final result before normalizing any finding.

      Enforce elapsed time in the compiler host. On expiry, request provider
      cancellation, reject any late final result, and settle the evaluation as
      incomplete without accepting partial observations as findings.

      If cancellation is already requested, do not invoke the provider. During a run,
      serialize cancellation, elapsed-time expiry, preventive budget exhaustion,
      process failure, and valid process settlement so the first observed terminal
      transition wins. Any non-settlement terminal requests provider termination and
      rejects a later result. Before returning that failure, terminate or detach the
      owned provider process under host cleanup policy and close or cancel every
      designated output channel.

      Invoke each provider once. Do not retry process launch, execution, protocol, or
      result failures inside the adapter.

      Apply preventive model budgets only when AdapterObservabilityDeclaration marks
      their enforcement preflight or live. For post-settlement-only or unavailable
      enforcement, compare final telemetry when available; an observed excess makes
      the evaluation incomplete but is recorded as detected rather than prevented.

      Preflight budget exhaustion starts no provider process. Live budget exhaustion
      participates in terminal-transition ordering after invocation.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    CapabilityPolicy {
      Compare the complete adapter declaration with the resolved
      AgentCapabilityContract from AgentEvaluationRequest. Use its closed value
      domains and exact-equality rule; unknown or omitted values mismatch.

      Adapter capability declarations are self-reported compatibility metadata. The
      compiler may compare them with requested capabilities but does not verify that
      an adapter implementation or provider CLI enforces them.

      Explicitly selecting or supplying an adapter accepts its documented execution
      risk. The compiler emits no generic adapter-risk log, event, report field,
      diagnostic, status effect, or evaluator-prompt content.

      A declared capability mismatch fails the required evaluation rather than
      silently broadening the requested behavior.
    }

    ProviderMapping {
      Codex, Claude, OpenCode, and Pi are the complete recognized provider identity
      set. Provider-specific command lines, event names, result locations, and
      telemetry fields remain private adapter details.

      Map raw provider output to AgentEvaluationRequest observation and framing
      vocabulary without exposing provider event identities to compiler consumers.

      Structural final-result failure rejects the complete result. Structurally valid
      candidate findings remain untrusted until CompilationDiagnostic rule and
      evidence normalization.

      Recompute AgentBudgetOutcome from the authoritative request and returned
      telemetry before accepting the adapter result. Reject a mismatched supplied
      outcome as final-result-protocol failure.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ProviderNeutralEvaluation {
      Decision: Let the compiler own evaluation requests, final-result validation,
      diagnostic normalization, and native skill-package contracts while adapters
      privately map those contracts to provider CLIs.

      Scope: Governs every agent and evaluation skill integration.

      Trade-offs: Provider-specific invocation, observation, result, and telemetry
      behavior requires a separate version-sensitive adapter mapping.

      Consequences: Changing providers does not change compiler diagnostic semantics.
    }

    ClosedProviderIdentitySet {
      Decision: Recognize exactly Codex, Claude, OpenCode, and Pi provider identities
      while permitting their adapter implementations to come from the compiler host
      or plugins.

      Scope: Governs compiler evaluator identity and adapter resolution; excludes
      arbitrary additional providers.

      Trade-offs: The provider contract remains predictable, but adding another CLI
      requires an explicit compiler contract change.

      Consequences: Plugins extend implementation availability without extending the
      semantic provider namespace.
    }

    ExplicitAdapterImplementationBinding {
      Decision: Bind each evaluator to one stable implementation identifier and exact
      implementation version in addition to its provider identity.

      Scope: Governs effective-profile identity and host or plugin adapter
      registration.

      Trade-offs: Adapter upgrades require an intentional configuration or resolved
      profile change.

      Design issues addressed: Provider identity alone is ambiguous when host and
      plugin implementations coexist.

      Discarded alternatives: Discovery order and implicit plugin-over-built-in
      precedence were rejected as nonreproducible.

      Consequences: Zero or multiple exact registration matches fail before provider
      invocation, and implementation identity participates in the profile
      fingerprint.
    }

    ExternalAdapterTrustBoundary {
      Decision: Treat adapter capabilities as self-declared and make explicit adapter
      selection the user's acceptance of documented execution risk.

      Scope: Governs compiler-owned and plugin-supplied adapters, provider CLI
      invocation, capability matching, and risk disclosure.

      Assumptions: Adapter and CLI implementations may execute with permissions
      available to their host environment.

      Trade-offs: The compiler cannot guarantee read-only access, network isolation,
      ephemeral state, command restrictions, or safe data handling.

      Design issues addressed: Plugin implementations cannot be truthfully certified
      from partial or terminal-only provider events.

      Discarded alternatives: Inferring enforcement from events and emitting a
      generic runtime warning were rejected as unreliable and as potential compiler
      context contamination.

      Consequences: Trust limitations are documented only. Logs, events, reports,
      diagnostics, status, and evaluator prompts contain no generic risk warning.
    }

    SingleAttemptExecution {
      Decision: Invoke one provider process once per adapter evaluation and leave any
      retry decision to the caller as a new evaluation.

      Scope: Governs launch, execution, protocol, and result failures inside an
      adapter invocation.

      Trade-offs: Transient failures are visible instead of automatically recovered.

      Design issues addressed: Hidden retries can repeat cost and externally supplied
      adapter behavior while mixing observations from distinct attempts.

      Discarded alternatives: Automatic launch, process, protocol, and result retries
      inside one evaluation were rejected.

      Consequences: Each evaluation has one process attempt, one terminal ordering,
      and unambiguous budget accounting.
    }

    FirstObservedTerminalTransition {
      Decision: Serialize cancellation, elapsed-time expiry, preventive budget
      exhaustion, process failure, and valid process settlement, and accept the first
      observed terminal transition.

      Scope: Governs races after one provider invocation begins.

      Trade-offs: A nearly simultaneous valid result can lose to an already observed
      cancellation or limit, while a later cancellation cannot revoke an accepted
      result.

      Design issues addressed: Provider callbacks and cancellation signals can race
      without a shared provider-independent timestamp.

      Discarded alternatives: Always preferring cancellation, limits, or successful
      settlement was rejected because each can overwrite an earlier terminal fact.

      Consequences: One serialized transition determines settlement and every later
      terminal signal or result is ignored.
    }

    CompleteProviderStreamSettlement {
      Decision: Accept a valid result only after provider termination and designated
      output end-of-stream, while allowing an earlier non-result terminal transition
      to fix the outcome before bounded provider cleanup completes.

      Scope: Governs owned provider processes, output channels, result parsing, and
      post-invocation adapter settlement.

      Trade-offs: A provider or descendant retaining a channel can require forced
      cleanup, and late valid output cannot replace an earlier failure winner.

      Design issues addressed: Settling a result before output closure leaves
      trailing duplicate or malformed results ambiguous, while waiting to select
      cancellation or timeout until cleanup completes makes them race unpredictably.

      Consequences: Terminal arbitration chooses one outcome first; cleanup prevents
      owned process or channel leakage; complete-stream validation is required only
      for a successful result.
    }

  }

  cases {
    AgentAdapter {
      A selected adapter declaring an incompatible requested capability fails before
      evaluation without implying that matching declarations are independently
      verified.

      Zero or two registrations matching one evaluator's provider, implementation
      identifier, and version fail before provider invocation.

      A terminal-only adapter returns a valid final findings object without progress
      observations and can complete successfully.

      Truncated command output with an intact valid final result can complete
      successfully.

      An adapter with no usage or cost fields reports unavailable telemetry rather
      than zero values.

      A configured nonpreventive token limit with final token usage within its limit
      and unavailable cost telemetry produces token within-limit and the independent
      cost outcome required by whether a cost limit is configured.

      Partial or unavailable telemetry for a configured nonpreventive limit produces
      indeterminate. Either final token or cost excess produces exceeded and makes
      the evaluation incomplete regardless of the other outcome.

      A structurally invalid final result rejects the evaluation.

      An undeclared diagnostic code is rejected before it can affect compilation
      color.

      Two terminal result candidates, or valid result content followed by malformed
      framing, reject the provider result without accepting either candidate.

      Elapsed-time expiry cancels provider work when possible, ignores a late final
      result, and leaves the evaluation incomplete.

      Cancellation requested before invocation starts no provider process.

      Preflight budget exhaustion starts no provider process and returns the
      preventive-budget failure.

      Cancellation serialized before process settlement wins, requests termination,
      and rejects a later final result; completed process settlement serialized first
      remains authoritative.

      Preventive budget exhaustion serialized before process settlement requests
      termination and rejects a later final result; valid process settlement
      serialized first remains authoritative.

      A provider process failure rejects the single attempt without retry.

      A final token or cost total exceeding a post-settlement-only budget leaves the
      evaluation incomplete and records that the excess was detected but not
      prevented.
    }
  }
}
